{In the second game Mr. Gwinner played the Queen's Gambit. White was somewhat
more tardy in development than Black and more hasty in attack; but he had a
perfectly sound game when, on the 16th move, he was intimidated by a sound but
really unproductive sacrifice on the part of his opponent, and permitted a
position to ensue which in three more moves induced him to resign.} 1. Nf3 Nf6
2. d4 d5 3. e3 e6 4. c4 b6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Bd3 O-O 7. O-O Bb7 8. Ne5 c5 9. Ne2
Nc6 10. f4 Rc8 11. b3 dxc4 12. Nxc6 Bxc6 13. bxc4 Ne4 14. f5 exf5 15. Rxf5 cxd4
16. exd4 Nc3 17. Nxc3 Qxd4+ 18. Kh1 Qxc3 19. Bf4 Rcd8 {Sources: Morning Post,
30 March 1885; The Chess Monthly 1884-1885 v6. Contributed by Gerard Killoran,
27 Feb 2019} 0-1

