1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. f4 exf4 ({Mr. Sherrard might have gained the
superiority here by} 4... Bxg1 5. Rxg1 Qh4+ {but as he neglected the
opportunity, he had the worse game after...}) 5. Nf3 d6 6. d4 Bb4 7. Bd2 ({
At this very point, however, Mr. Walker left off his opponent by 7 Bd2 instead
of} 7. Bxf4) 7... Bg4 8. a3 Bxc3 9. Bxc3 Qe7 10. O-O O-O-O 11. Qd3 Nf6 12. Ng5
Rhf8 13. Rxf4 h6 14. Nh3 g5 15. Rff1 Nxe4 16. Rae1 d5 (16... d5 {Mr. Walker
might have regained the pawn by} 17. Bxd5 Rxd5 18. Rxe4 {with a good game -
GEW.}) 17. Bb5 Qd7 18. Bb4 Nd6 19. Rf6 Bxh3 20. Bxc6 Qxc6 21. Qxh3+ Qd7 22.
Qxh6 Rh8 23. Qxg5 Ne4 24. Rxe4 dxe4 25. Qe3 Qxd4 26. Qxd4 Rxd4 27. Rxf7 e3 28.
Kf1 Rxh2 29. Rf8+ Rd8 30. Rf3 Re8 31. Kg1 Rh5 32. Bc3 Rd5 {adjudicated win for
Black. Source: The Chess-Monthly, Vol.6, April 1885, p245} 0-1

